    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "One year later, Tesla and Musk still don&#8217;t have a good definition of &#8216;abundance&#8217;",
      "d": "The CEO promised to get more specific about his vision. But the details are still absent.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/01/one-year-later-tesla-and-musk-are-still-dont-have-a-good-definition-of-abundance/",
      "m": "10. 01. PM 03:50",
      "ts": 1790869810000,
      "fullText": "On September 1, 2025, Elon Musk laid out his fourth &#8220; Master Plan &#8221; for Tesla, which was immediately criticized by some of his most ardent fans for being generic . Musk agreed and promised to add more specifics. They never came. But that hasn&#8217;t stopped Musk and other Tesla executives from spending much of the past year promising that the company&#8217;s many futuristic efforts &#8212; robotaxis, robots, AI &#8212; are all part of a grander machine that will create an era of &#8220;amazing abundance.&#8221; What that means, more than a year later, is still tricky to pin down. The topline promises of the plan have remained big and bawdy even as its tagline has changed from sustainable abundance — a clearer connection to the company&#8217;s former mission — to the &#8220;amazing abundance&#8221; slogan, which Musk chose because it was &#8220;more joyful.&#8221; Musk has talked about a far-flung future where robots and AI do surgery and increase access to medical care. He has said the proliferation of AI and robotics means that &#8220;everyone can have a penthouse if they want.&#8221; He (virtually) shouted &#8220;Amazing abundance for all!&#8221; in a quote-tweet of a post from Midjourney founder David Holz, who had claimed 5 million humanoid robots could &#8220;build Manhattan in ~6 months.&#8221; In July, Musk told Economist editor-in-chief Zanny Minton Beddoes that amazing abundance means &#8220;anyone can have anything they can think of.&#8221; He also mentioned on Joe Rogan&#8217;s podcast late last year that &#8220;sustainable abundance&#8221; would mean &#8220;we haven’t destroyed nature&#8221; or &#8220;paved the rainforest&#8221; and we still &#8220;have the national parks,&#8221; despite supporting two politicians &#8212; Donald Trump and Jair Bolsonaro &#8212; who have actively worked against those goals. He appears to have only made this particular claim once, though. Practical details are basically nonexistent, though. Does Musk really th"
    },
